 ## Overview
-The Phong Reflection Model describes how light is reflected from object surfaces, defining the color of each pixel as the sum of ambient, diffuse, and specular components [S1, unofficial]. It calculates the amount and direction of light falling on an object using surface normals, light source positions, and the viewer's eye point [S2]. Unlike its sibling [Phong Shading](1853-phong-shading.md), which interpolates normals across a polygon, this method computes lighting per-voxel or per-pixel using the local geometry at the hit point. It builds on [Simplified Phong Lighting Model](885-simplified-phong-lighting-model.md) by explicitly separating the specular calculation into a Blinn-Phong half-vector formulation [S1, unofficial].
+The Phong Reflection Model describes how light is reflected from objects, defining the color of each pixel as the sum of ambient, diffuse, and specular components [S1, unofficial]. It calculates the amount and direction of light falling on an object's surface using normals, light sources, and the viewer's eye point [S2]. It builds on [Simplified Phong Lighting Model](885-simplified-phong-lighting-model.md) by explicitly separating the specular calculation into a Blinn-Phong half-vector formulation [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## How it works
 **Inputs and Outputs**
-*   **Inputs**: Surface normal $\mathbf{N}$ at the hit point; light direction vector $\mathbf{L}$ (from surface to light); view direction vector $\mathbf{V}$ (from surface to camera); light color/intensity $I_{light}$; ambient light color/intensity $I_{ambient}$; object diffuse color coefficient $k_d$; object specular color coefficient $k_s$; specular power/shininess $k$ (or $n$).
