 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity**: $O(1)$ per voxel/pixel. The calculation involves a constant number of vector operations (dot products, normalization, exponentiation).
-*   **Memory Complexity**: $O(1)$ auxiliary memory. No precomputed tables or large buffers are required.
-*   **Strengths**: Computationally inexpensive; provides a visually plausible approximation of shiny surfaces; easy to implement [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations**: The Blinn-Phong specular term is an approximation and does not strictly conserve energy or follow physical BRDFs as accurately as microfacet models. Specular highlights may appear overly broad or sharp depending on the power parameter, lacking the precise falloff of physical models [S2].
-*   **When to use**: Real-time rendering, volume visualization where performance is critical, or when a simple "shiny" look is desired without the cost of more complex models.
-*   **When not to use**: Physically based rendering (PBR) requiring strict energy conservation, or when accurate specular distribution is critical for scientific accuracy.
 
 ## Background
-The Phong Reflection Model was introduced by Bui Tuong Phong in 1975 [S1, unofficial][S2]. It became a standard in computer graphics for shading surfaces. The specific implementation described in the sources uses the Blinn-Phong variation for the specular term, which uses the half-vector instead of the reflection vector, offering computational efficiency and smoother results [S1, unofficial]. It is widely used in visualization tools like Data Explorer, where it is implemented in the "Shade" module to control the appearance of volume data [S2].
